    "title": "East Lansing police oversight commissioners challenge chief - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Todd Heywood ,\n1 hours ago\nEAST LANSING, Mich. (WLNS) — East Lansing Independent Police Oversight commissioners challenged city police chief Jennifer Brown’s decision to not investigate two officers involved in a pepper spray incident in August at a Wednesday meeting.\nAt issue was a one-sentence message in an email response to ELIPOC Vice Chair Kath Edsall by Brown.\n“Per the attorney, investigation of these complaints are on hold due to pending litigation,” the chief wrote in response to an inquiry about the complaint numbers for the formal ELIPOC complaints against the two officers.\nEmails between ELPD Chief Jennifer Brown and ELIPOC Vice Chair Kath Edsall regarding complaints authorized by the Commission in November. (Screen shot of agenda and package from city of East Lansing. WLNS) “The reply that you had was that you had been advised by legal to not be those investigations. So I’m wondering if that means you are above the law,” asked Kath Edsall, ELIPOC vice chair. “The law requires the ordinance requires that those investment investigation complaint is filed. That is to be investigated by the department. So, are you being told by a legal that you are above the law, that you do not have to follow the law?”\nChief Jennifer Brown at the Dec. 14, 2026 meeting of the East Lansing Independent Police Oversight Commission. (WLNS) Brown responded, “What I’m being told by legal is exactly what I told you in the e-mail and that we are not going to investigate those.”\n“That’s the question. So, you are above the law? And I’m asking seriously,” Edsall continued. “The law says you need to investigate complaints filed.”\nCommissioners approved resolutions to file formal complaints against two officers involved in an Aug. 24 pepper-spray incident in front Dave’s Hot Chicken in Downtown East Lansing.\nPolice originally released body cam footage from the incident and said the use of the chemical was the result of a fight. But surveillance video from the restaurant released by an attorney for one of the young Black men told a different story .\nCriminal charges against both men were dismissed , and they subsequently filed federal civil rights lawsuits against the city, Brown and Officer Andrew Lyons.\nThe commission has also sought to have the city council and the city manager hold Brown accountable for her role in the situation.\n“Due to pending litigation, the City of East Lansing has no comment,” wrote City Communications Director Carrie Sampson in an email response to an inquiry about the ELIPOC resolution requests regarding Brown.\nBoth Edsall and Ernest Conerly, chair of the commission, said the city’s ordinance that empowers the commission also requires ELPD to investigate complaints.\n“The city of East Lansing or maybe the police department, likes to think that laws are suggestions when it comes to the work that they do,” Conerly said. “Everybody else must follow them. But for them, it’s a suggestion.”\nAs Brown was leaving Wednesday’s meeting, 6 News Investigates attempted to get answers from her. Asked if there were other complaints that had not been investigated by ELPD, Brown said she didn’t know and refused to answer additional questions.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.",
